Connect slurry transport with measured rheology, particles, and route conditions (@mine-slurry: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mining-and-mineral-engineering, #source "../sources/Introduction-to-Mineral-Processing-NPTEL.lingua#r_5VQ67PA1160RQ1EWN17211XBWD", #source "../sources/Tratamento-de-Minerios-CETEM.lingua#r_04ZQFADZ3NC3T6QDPQ565W1ERA", #source "../sources/CETEM-Praticas-Laboratoriais.lingua#r_44AAWXB1D7MBR9B0V3TYB8GF9B", #mining-and-mineral-engineering "Stockpiles-blending-segregation-and-material-identity.lingua#r_0S490SF4SPP602PF43TKA0QDWY") { r_099JPP0YGKZAWN4T14QQ3QB80C
**Read:** [Introduction to Mineral Processing — Arun Kumar Majumder, NPTEL](../sources/Introduction-to-Mineral-Processing-NPTEL.lingua#r_5VQ67PA1160RQ1EWN17211XBWD), the complete slurry-transport lectures; [Tratamento de Minérios — CETEM, sixth edition](../sources/Tratamento-de-Minerios-CETEM.lingua#r_04ZQFADZ3NC3T6QDPQ565W1ERA), the full relevant slurry, pumping, and rheology discussions; [Tratamento de Minérios: Práticas Laboratoriais — Sampaio, França, and Braga](../sources/CETEM-Praticas-Laboratoriais.lingua#r_44AAWXB1D7MBR9B0V3TYB8GF9B), the complete material-density, concentration, and flow-test methods used.

An industrial slurry may settle, segregate, exhibit yield behavior or shear dependence, and change with water chemistry or preparation. A solids concentration without particle distribution and density does not completely describe transport. Record temperature, shear history, test geometry, and the basis of each rheological model.

Convert mass and volume fractions using explicit solid/liquid densities. For an invented nonporous solid of 2.7 t/m³ in water of 1.0 t/m³ at 60% dry solids by mass, the mixture density is 1/(0.6/2.7 + 0.4/1.0) = 45/28 t/m³, about 1.60714. The solid volume fraction is 5/14, about 0.35714. Entrained gas or different composition changes this model.

Pressure loss, elevation, velocity, wear, pump performance, deposition, and start/stop behavior belong to one transport account. A water-only friction relation or a laboratory viscosity at one shear condition may fail outside its domain. Compare alternatives with the actual operating envelope and maintenance observations.

**Work:** analyze a complete slurry transport study. Reproduce concentration conversions and the chosen pressure/work model, compare with independent measurements across conditions, and investigate an alternative settling or yield interpretation. Close water/solids through the transport interfaces and explain how interruption, feed change, or an instrument bias would be detected. Preserve actual limits rather than publishing one universal transport velocity.

**Continue:** [Model storage and blending as changing material populations and inventories](Stockpiles-blending-segregation-and-material-identity.lingua#r_0S490SF4SPP602PF43TKA0QDWY).

